Here's a C++ function that takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <sstream>
#include <regex>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html;
    std::regex pattern(R"(^\[.*?\])");
    std::smatch match;

    while (std::regex_search(markdown, match, pattern)) {
        std::string text = match[1];
        html += "<p>" + text + "</p>";
    }

    return html;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "[This is a paragraph]\n[Another]";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function uses a regular expression to match markdown text enclosed in square brackets (`[text]`) and replaces it with an HTML paragraph (`<p>`). The `std::regex_search` function is used to find all matches in the input string, and the `match[1]` expression extracts the text inside the square brackets. The resulting HTML string is constructed and returned by the function.

